hi I'm Nick Gillespie with Matt Welch  we're doing ask a libertarian 2012 to  celebrate the paperback publication of  our 2011 book the Declaration of  Independence that has a new forward  please pre-order it at your bookstore  online next question from Franklin  Carter via email  what's the libertarian solution to crony  capitalism and if it is possible to  abolish crony capitalism  how do people prevent it from coming  back well you're never gonna abolish  crony capitalism but the best method for  limiting it is by limiting government  this is counterintuitive to a lot of  people so slow it down  think about the record number of  lobbyists lobbying in Washington DC when  did that happen  well that's happened under the Barack  Obama administration which came into  office saying we're gonna reduce the  power of lobbyists well they did that at  the same time while spending record  amounts of money and writing huge bits  of legislation in the form of the  Affordable Care Act in the dodd-frank  bill which kicks a lot of things to  regulators and when you do that you are  inviting industry to come in and write  their own legislation the more  regulation you have the more crony  capitalism you'll have and the more  deregulation you have the less one  example that we talked about at great  length in the book is the deregulation  of the airline industry for four decades  the airline industry from its inception  in the late 30s through the early 70s  was the same for Airlines it was crony  capitalism perfectly managed they got to  determine who entered the market who you  know flew this route between these two  cities with the help of the government  well when the government caught out of  that business suddenly those big guys  had to compete they didn't like it  everyone else did especially at  including the consumers if you want to  help crony capitalism limit the amount  of big government activity and you know  the one thing that we can do without  minimizing the way special interest  politics works by creating a populace  and a voting electorate that is  interested and you know totally  frustrated by crony capitalism we can  reduce it we can get rid of politicians  who consistently vote against broad  interests in favor of narrow special  interests and that's part of what we try  to do in the book moving away from the  sort of stuff to actual capitalism to  free-market decentralized power  and innovation is the way to reduce this  going forward ask a libertarian 2.0 keep  those questions coming we'll be here all  day see you soon
 

